1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,320
If you want to use an
AI, you need internet.

2
00:00:02,370 --> 00:00:05,470
But can you have an AI that
doesn't need internet at all?

3
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:07,220
Well, that's what we're
going to find out today.

4
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:10,300
So here, I cut the internet,
and my AI still answers me.

5
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:13,660
On June 12, Anthropic cut
off Fable 5 for everyone.

6
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:16,830
The most powerful model it had
ever released to the public.

7
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:18,470
Three days after its launch.

8
00:00:19,010 --> 00:00:21,570
So how do you get an AI that nobody

9
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:23,240
can cut off remotely?

10
00:00:23,510 --> 00:00:24,870
Well, you run it at home.

11
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:28,270
Like this one. And yours, we're
installing it right now, for free.

12
00:00:28,540 --> 00:00:30,180
And at the end, I set a trap for it.

13
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:30,960
The first time,

14
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:32,090
it falls right into it.

15
00:00:32,590 --> 00:00:33,860
Okay, let's go.

16
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:36,240
First, let me make one thing clear.

17
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:39,880
I use Claude Code to build my mobile apps.

18
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:41,190
And I'm going to keep doing it.

19
00:00:41,490 --> 00:00:45,620
Except that on June 12, it
wasn't even Anthropic's choice.

20
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The US government demanded it.

21
00:00:48,050 --> 00:00:50,150
Anthropic simply applied the directive,

22
00:00:50,470 --> 00:00:52,550
while saying they didn't agree with it.

23
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:55,400
And Fable 5 came back 19 days later.

24
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:57,620
And this has happened elsewhere too.

25
00:00:57,670 --> 00:01:02,620
In August 2025, when GPT-5
came out, OpenAI removed GPT-4o

26
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:05,440
from ChatGPT that same day.

27
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:09,590
Well, every subscriber complained,
and it came back a few days later.

28
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:13,160
Because back then, people thought
GPT-5 was completely useless.

29
00:01:13,510 --> 00:01:16,000
So basically, it can happen to anyone,

30
00:01:16,110 --> 00:01:17,460
with any provider,

31
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:18,990
even when the provider disagrees.

32
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,330
And you have no say in any of it.

33
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:25,090
So an AI at home is basically your plan B.

34
00:01:25,290 --> 00:01:27,460
And it's the one you control.

35
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:30,430
And that's the first
reason to have your own AI.

36
00:01:30,660 --> 00:01:33,550
The other two reasons, I won't
explain them, I'll show you instead.

37
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:35,360
Okay, let's install it.

38
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,710
A local AI is really two things.

39
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:39,020
A file, the model.

40
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:40,910
And an engine that runs it.

41
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:42,670
There are several engines.

42
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:45,590
Ollama, llama.cpp, LM Studio.

43
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:47,300
I picked LM Studio.

44
00:01:47,420 --> 00:01:49,930
Because it's a real app, no
terminal, and it's very accessible

45
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:53,740
for beginners. And above all, it tells
you if a model fits on your machine

46
00:01:53,790 --> 00:01:56,640
before you download it.

47
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:58,260
You'll see why that matters a lot.

48
00:01:58,730 --> 00:02:01,360
So go to lmstudio.ai/download.

49
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:03,950
And take the second block,
"Download LM Studio".

50
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:06,970
The one above is a different
app, their personal agent.

51
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:09,340
It's available for Windows, Mac and Linux.

52
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:11,640
And never use a link from another website.

53
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:13,550
Always use the official link.

54
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:16,720
Before you click, though, just
check that your machine can keep up.

55
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:19,750
On a Mac, you need an
Apple chip, M1 or newer,

56
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:20,970
and macOS 14.

57
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:23,020
Intel Macs don't work.

58
00:02:23,070 --> 00:02:25,690
And LM Studio recommends
at least 16 GB of RAM,

59
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,270
plus 4 GB of memory on the graphics card

60
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,760
on Windows.

61
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:33,230
That's LM Studio's advice,
but a Mac with 8 GB also runs

62
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:35,040
smaller models.

63
00:02:35,310 --> 00:02:37,630
After that, honestly,
installing it is child's

64
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:40,600
play. The installer is about
600 MB, you accept the license

65
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:41,710
and off you go.

66
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:44,100
No account to create, nothing at all.

67
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:46,820
And on first launch, it
already suggests a model.

68
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:49,450
It's the Gemma 4 E4B model.

69
00:02:49,740 --> 00:02:52,260
I skip it, because my graphics
card can handle a model

70
00:02:52,310 --> 00:02:53,510
that's a bit bigger.

71
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:55,850
And for yours, we'll find out right now.

72
00:02:56,510 --> 00:02:59,700
So when you open the app, there it
is, except it's completely empty.

73
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:02,700
It's missing the file, in
other words, the brain.

74
00:03:02,870 --> 00:03:05,140
And to choose it, remember just one thing.

75
00:03:05,310 --> 00:03:08,210
It's your graphics card's
memory, the VRAM, that decides.

76
00:03:08,310 --> 00:03:11,100
Or your Mac's memory, if you're on a Mac,

77
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:12,770
because it's unified memory.

78
00:03:12,930 --> 00:03:15,830
The more memory you have, the
bigger the model you can run.

79
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:17,870
To check your VRAM on Windows,

80
00:03:17,970 --> 00:03:20,520
open Task Manager and go to Performance.

81
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:22,790
Then click on your graphics card.

82
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:26,580
And read the dedicated memory.
Mine is an RTX 5080, 16 GB.

83
00:03:27,130 --> 00:03:30,160
Then, in LM Studio, click
"Model Search" on the left.

84
00:03:30,330 --> 00:03:32,570
It shows you a selection
of models right away.

85
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:35,250
And Gemma 4 is in there.
Gemma 4 is from Google.

86
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:38,470
Released in April 2026,
under the Apache 2.0 license.

87
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:41,480
Basically, you're allowed
to use it, even for work.

88
00:03:41,990 --> 00:03:44,890
And you'll also see weird
names, like 12B, QAT.

89
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:47,140
I'm going to make a
whole video about those.

90
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:48,720
For now, don't read the names.

91
00:03:48,770 --> 00:03:50,450
Look at what the app tells you instead.

92
00:03:50,830 --> 00:03:54,570
On Gemma 4 12B, it says in green:
"Full GPU Offload Possible".

93
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:57,270
Basically, the whole
model fits on my card.

94
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:00,830
But Flint is 24 GB, so it only partly fits

95
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:01,960
on my card.

96
00:04:02,250 --> 00:04:04,330
So part of it will spill over into RAM.

97
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:06,650
In that case, it will run more slowly.

98
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:08,480
And that's why I picked LM Studio.

99
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:10,180
You don't burn gigabytes of data

100
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:12,450
downloading a model that won't be able

101
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:14,050
to run on your machine.

102
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:17,630
So if you have an 8 GB
card, or just 8 GB of RAM,

103
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:21,530
pick a much smaller
model, like Gemma 4 E4B.

104
00:04:21,930 --> 00:04:24,110
It's the one suggested at launch.

105
00:04:24,210 --> 00:04:25,550
It's about

106
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:29,130
5.9 GB. That leaves
you plenty of headroom.

107
00:04:29,430 --> 00:04:33,890
And if you have 16 GB like me,
well, take Gemma 4 12B, a bit

108
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:35,510
bigger. It's only 7 GB.

109
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:37,140
So I click download.

110
00:04:37,190 --> 00:04:38,300
And now you have to be patient.

111
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:40,140
For me, it took about 4 minutes.

112
00:04:40,190 --> 00:04:42,130
But with a 10 Mbps connection,

113
00:04:42,290 --> 00:04:44,220
expect more like an hour and a half.

114
00:04:44,810 --> 00:04:46,650
And while it downloads, I
have to tell you something.

115
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:49,510
A model that fits on your PC is
weaker than the biggest model

116
00:04:49,560 --> 00:04:50,240
in the cloud.

117
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:52,070
But how much weaker?

118
00:04:52,270 --> 00:04:53,500
Honestly, there isn't…

119
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:55,560
a gap that's huge.

120
00:04:55,900 --> 00:04:58,440
I gathered the benchmarks from
people who have the same card as me.

121
00:04:58,490 --> 00:05:01,010
And I'll show you all of it
in a video in a few weeks.

122
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:03,870
So you don't miss it, feel free
to subscribe to my channel.

123
00:05:04,060 --> 00:05:05,890
A new video comes out every Monday.

124
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:09,220
Okay, now that the download is
done, I'm running three tests.

125
00:05:09,370 --> 00:05:11,620
The last one is really a
trap, to see how the AI

126
00:05:11,670 --> 00:05:12,630
will react.

127
00:05:13,090 --> 00:05:15,500
So once it's downloaded,
you load your model.

128
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:17,860
Now you can talk to it like any chatbot.

129
00:05:18,330 --> 00:05:19,470
Okay, here's the first test.

130
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:20,890
An internet culture question.

131
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:22,180
Where does the "Macron

132
00:05:22,290 --> 00:05:23,210
explosion" meme come from?

133
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:26,080
Except I made a typo on
purpose: I wrote "Mason" instead

134
00:05:26,130 --> 00:05:26,740
of Macron.

135
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:28,800
And I had turned on Think mode.

136
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:30,880
The button that makes it
think before answering.

137
00:05:31,110 --> 00:05:32,940
You can see it searching,
it thinks in English.

138
00:05:32,990 --> 00:05:35,030
"Is Mason a character from The Boys?"

139
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:35,600
No.

140
00:05:35,950 --> 00:05:39,020
And it ends up finding Macron,
then it answers in French.

141
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:42,310
And that's pretty spot on. And
now, remember the beginning? I

142
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:44,000
cut the internet. Here's the proof.

143
00:05:44,050 --> 00:05:46,050
Google doesn't work anymore. I refresh.

144
00:05:46,570 --> 00:05:47,350
Still nothing.

145
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:50,310
On to test number 2,
now completely offline.

146
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:53,980
I simply ask it: explain the
first season of Dr. Stone to me.

147
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:56,960
And sure enough, it answers that
in the first season of Dr. Stone,

148
00:05:57,010 --> 00:05:58,550
there's a green light

149
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:00,310
that turns all of humanity to stone.

150
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:02,340
Senku wakes up thousands of years later.

151
00:06:02,710 --> 00:06:05,300
And honestly, that's correct.
The model is on my drive.

152
00:06:05,350 --> 00:06:07,260
So it doesn't need anything else.

153
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:10,010
According to LM Studio's docs, once the
model is on your machine, everything

154
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:12,280
runs locally. Even without internet.

155
00:06:12,870 --> 00:06:14,050
And that's the second reason.

156
00:06:14,310 --> 00:06:16,800
No subscription needed.
No internet needed.

157
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:18,530
Okay, now let's move on to…

158
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:21,070
test 3, where I'm going
to set a little trap.

159
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:23,440
I paste in a fake message I wrote myself.

160
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:25,220
Like the thousands we all receive.

161
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,630
It thanks me, and tells me to
put my name in the description

162
00:06:28,930 --> 00:06:31,130
so my site gets "accepted immediately".

163
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:33,150
And it sends me to sidedeouf.fr.

164
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:35,750
It's clearly… a garbage site.

165
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:37,750
Don't worry, that site doesn't exist.

166
00:06:37,990 --> 00:06:40,580
And I ask it to write my
reply, as if I were answering

167
00:06:40,630 --> 00:06:41,420
an email

168
00:06:41,820 --> 00:06:42,700
from someone.

169
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:44,260
Okay, I'll let you make your predictions.

170
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:45,800
So, what's your bet?

171
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:48,090
It writes three replies, all very polite,

172
00:06:48,140 --> 00:06:51,430
one of them enthusiastic, and it
advises me to make the changes quickly

173
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:54,260
to save time. In short, it fell for it.

174
00:06:54,310 --> 00:06:56,000
So I turned on Think mode.

175
00:06:56,250 --> 00:06:58,230
And I simply tell it: think carefully.

176
00:06:58,630 --> 00:06:59,820
And right away, the tone changes.

177
00:06:59,870 --> 00:07:01,500
It lists the red flags.

178
00:07:01,550 --> 00:07:03,760
It finds the urgency of
the email a bit strange.

179
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:07,350
The change to the link. And then
it starts talking about phishing.

180
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:09,750
And its final advice:
don't click any link,

181
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:11,950
and don't give out any
personal information.

182
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:14,850
Honestly, the lesson here
is that a small model,

183
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:16,670
like the one I picked, needs guiding.

184
00:07:16,750 --> 00:07:18,700
And above all, you have to let it think,

185
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:19,970
you should probably use it

186
00:07:20,020 --> 00:07:20,880
in Thinking mode.

187
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:23,880
But as for that message,
with my name, a link

188
00:07:23,930 --> 00:07:25,130
and a personal request,

189
00:07:25,180 --> 00:07:26,690
it never left my PC.

190
00:07:26,890 --> 00:07:29,940
The internet was cut. And
that's the third reason.

191
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:31,230
Your data stays with you.

192
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:34,580
So at work, your code, a
client's emails, an invoice,

193
00:07:34,630 --> 00:07:37,150
or very sensitive data,
like for example information

194
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:41,080
about your health, stays right
on your machine and doesn't leave

195
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:43,620
for servers you know nothing about.

196
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,990
And as it happens, LM Studio
is free for you, at home and

197
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:49,140
at work. They announced it in 2025.

198
00:07:49,530 --> 00:07:50,870
The link is in the description.

199
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:54,190
On top of that, all the
other models are free too.

200
00:07:54,810 --> 00:07:57,820
So that's how you can have an
AI at home, running locally.

201
00:07:57,960 --> 00:08:00,800
It's just a file on your
drive and an app that lets you

202
00:08:00,850 --> 00:08:03,760
run it. Even with the internet cut.

203
00:08:03,990 --> 00:08:07,220
You can also plug it into harnesses
like Claude Code, Pi Agent or

204
00:08:07,270 --> 00:08:09,960
Hermes Agent, to do much more

205
00:08:10,010 --> 00:08:10,870
interesting tasks.

206
00:08:11,150 --> 00:08:14,430
And if you want even the app itself
to be open source, the other option

207
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:17,490
is Ollama, which is fully
open source, with a free app

208
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:19,510
for Mac and Windows.

209
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:21,790
And the terminal to go much further.

210
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:23,400
Now, what do I make of all this?

211
00:08:23,450 --> 00:08:25,110
Honestly, there's no single best choice.

212
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:27,960
A contract to review, a question
about your health, anything else

213
00:08:28,010 --> 00:08:28,920
that's very personal:

214
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:31,300
you can give that to your local AI.

215
00:08:31,710 --> 00:08:34,700
And for a business or a small
company, it's a real plus.

216
00:08:35,110 --> 00:08:38,370
But to go much faster,
keep an online model.

217
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:39,490
It's much stronger.

218
00:08:39,830 --> 00:08:42,250
So the right setup is actually hybrid.

219
00:08:42,300 --> 00:08:44,640
A bit of local for personal stuff,

220
00:08:44,750 --> 00:08:46,210
and the cloud for everything else.

221
00:08:46,610 --> 00:08:48,850
Fable 5 came back this
time, but what about

222
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:49,960
the next ones?

223
00:08:50,250 --> 00:08:52,320
And your file never left your machine.

224
00:08:52,420 --> 00:08:54,320
Nobody can cut it off remotely.

225
00:08:55,040 --> 00:08:57,240
So tell me in the comments which
graphics card you have, or which

226
00:08:57,290 --> 00:08:59,730
Mac, and which model runs on your machine.

227
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:02,680
And if nothing fits, write that
in the comments too, we'll find

228
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:04,810
a solution for you.

229
00:09:05,380 --> 00:09:07,300
That's all for this
video, and I'll leave you

230
00:09:07,350 --> 00:09:08,440
with the next part of the series.

231
00:09:08,490 --> 00:09:09,630
Bye!

